      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Let the two numbers be \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). We are given two pieces of information:\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6: \\( x + y = 6 \\)\n2. The difference of their squares is 12: \\( x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\)\n\nWe can use the identity for the difference of squares, which states that \\( x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\). Substituting the given sum of the numbers into this identity, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) = 6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x - y \\), we divide both sides by 6:\n\\[\nx - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 6 \\)\n2. \\( x - y = 2 \\)\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations together:\n\\[\n(x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2\n\\]\n\\[\n2x = 8\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\( x = 4 \\) back into the first equation to find \\( y \\):\n\\[\n4 + y = 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the two numbers are 4 and 2. The positive difference between these two numbers is:\n\\[\n4 - 2 = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the positive difference of the two numbers is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
