      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers as \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). According to the problem, we have two key pieces of information:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers is 6:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 6\n   \\]\n\n2. The difference of their squares is 12:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - y^2 = 12\n   \\]\n\nWe can use the difference of squares formula, which states that \\( x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\). Substituting the first equation into this formula, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) = 6(x - y) = 12\n\\]\n\nNow, we can solve for \\( x - y \\):\n\\[\n6(x - y) = 12 \\implies x - y = \\frac{12}{6} = 2\n\\]\n\nThe positive difference between the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can find \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) using the values of \\( x + y = 6 \\) and \\( x - y = 2 \\). Adding these two equations, we get:\n\\[\n(x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2 \\implies 2x = 8 \\implies x = 4\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get:\n\\[\n(x + y) - (x - y) = 6 - 2 \\implies 2y = 4 \\implies y = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the two numbers are \\( x = 4 \\) and \\( y = 2 \\), and their positive difference is:\n\\[\n|4 - 2| = 2\n\\]\n\nThe solution is verified and the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
